Private instances must access object storage via a VPC endpoint due to a restrictive bucket policy. Symptoms: AccessDenied and NAT egress observed. Route table for the private subnet:
```json
{
  "Routes": [
    {"Destination": "10.0.0.0/16", "Target": "local"},
    {"Destination": "0.0.0.0/0",   "Target": "nat-0abc"}
  ],
  "Associations": ["subnet-priv-a", "subnet-priv-b"]
}
```
Gateway endpoint `vpce-gw-s3-123` exists but S3 traffic still goes to the NAT. What change corrects the path so requests hit the endpoint and the bucket policy?

Lifecycle configuration for a bucket holding user uploads:
```json
{
  "Rules": [{
    "ID": "immediate-archive",
    "Filter": {"Prefix": "uploads/"},
    "Transitions": [{"Days": 0, "StorageClass": "DEEP_ARCHIVE"}],
    "Status": "Enabled"
  }]
}
```
Right after upload, the app performs a GET to read back and process the object. What actually happens to those immediate GETs and why?

A nightly cross-account backup from the prod account (111111111111) to a central bucket in the backup account (555555555555) suddenly fails. Artefacts:

SCP at the org root:
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "DenyPutObjectToCentralBucket",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": "s3:PutObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::central-backup-bucket/*",
      "Condition": {"StringNotEquals": {"aws:PrincipalAccount": "555555555555"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

Backup role policy in prod:
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "s3:PutObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::central-backup-bucket/prod/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

CLI output:
```text
aws s3 cp db.dump s3://central-backup-bucket/prod/db.dump
upload failed: ... AccessDenied: blocked by SCP
```
What change unblocks the backup without weakening least privilege?

Cross-region replication with destination-side KMS encryption fails:
```json
{
  "Rule": "CRR-to-eu",
  "SSE-KMS": {"DestinationKeyArn": "arn:aws:kms:eu-west-1:2222:key/abcd"}
}
```
Error:
```text
KMS.AccessDenied: arn:aws:iam::1111:role/replication not authorized for kms:Encrypt on arn:aws:kms:eu-west-1:2222:key/abcd
```
Destination CMK policy:
```json
{
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "AllowUseFromBackupTeam",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::2222:role/backup"},
      "Action": ["kms:Encrypt","kms:Decrypt"],
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}
```
What change enables secure replication?

A backup role currently has:
```json
{
  "Statement": [
    {"Effect": "Allow", "Action": ["s3:ListBucket"], "Resource": "*"},
    {"Effect": "Allow", "Action": ["s3:GetObject","s3:PutObject"], "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::backup-*/*"}
  ]
}
```
To meet least-privilege, an engineer adds this to the ListBucket statement:
```json
"Condition": {"StringLike": {"s3:prefix": "backup-*"}}
```
What is the real effect?

Your Terraform defines an autoscaling policy on a compute group. Detailed monitoring is disabled (default 5-minute metrics). The policy is:
```terraform
resource "aws_autoscaling_policy" "cpu_scale_out" {
  name                   = "cpu-scale-out"
  autoscaling_group_name = aws_autoscaling_group.app.name
  policy_type            = "TargetTrackingScaling"
  target_tracking_configuration {
    predefined_metric_specification {
      predefined_metric_type = "ASGAverageCPUUtilization"
    }
    target_value       = 60
    disable_scale_in   = false
    # CloudWatch default unless detailed monitoring is on
    customized_metric_specification = null
  }
}
```
CloudWatch alarm history shows 1-minute spikes above 60% several times per hour, but no scale-out ever occurs. What explains this, and what change is most appropriate?

Your architecture places a CDN in front of a web tier. Load balancer health checks hit /health over GET. You see this NGINX config and CDN rule:
```nginx
location = /health {
  add_header Cache-Control "public, max-age=60";
  proxy_pass http://app;
}
```
```text
CDN rule: cache all GETs for 60s unless Cache-Control says no-store
```
What is the most likely effect on health checks if the upstream app goes down, and the best quick fix?
